A complete program that prints a greeting:

#include <stdio.h>

int main(void)
{
    printf("hello, world\n");
    return 0;
}

Here #include <stdio.h> pulls in printf, main is the entry point, printf writes text, \n is a newline, and return 0 signals success. A loop that counts to three:

for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++)
{
    printf("%i\n", i);
}

The loop declares i = 0, keeps going while i < 3, and does i++ (add one) each pass, printing 0, 1, 2. A condition:

if (score >= 90)
{
    printf("A\n");
}

The block runs only when the test in parentheses is true.

Declaring and reading an array in C:

int scores[3] = {90, 80, 70};
printf("%i\n", scores[0]);  // prints 90

The first element is scores[0], the last is scores[2]. A string and its length:

char word[] = "HI";   // stored as 'H','I','\0'
int n = strlen(word); // n is 2, the terminator is not counted

Looping over characters:

for (int i = 0; word[i] != '\0'; i++)
{
    printf("%c\n", word[i]);
}

The loop stops when it meets '\0'. Off-by-one alert: an array declared with size 3 has valid indices 0, 1, and 2 only.

A function with a parameter and a return value:

int square(int x)
{
    return x * x;
}

int main(void)
{
    printf("%i\n", square(5));  // prints 25
    return 0;
}

Calling square pushes a frame with x = 5; returning pops it and hands 25 back to main. A recursive function:

int factorial(int n)
{
    if (n <= 1) return 1;      // base case
    return n * factorial(n - 1);
}

factorial(3) calls factorial(2), which calls factorial(1); the base case returns 1, then the frames unwind to give 1*2*3 = 6. Each pending call sits in its own stack frame until its callee returns.

A linked-list node in C:

typedef struct node
{
    int value;
    struct node *next;
}
node;

Each node points to the next; the last node's next is NULL, marking the end. Hash idea: to store the word 'apple', compute hash("apple") to pick a bucket index, then place it there; to look it up later, hash the same word and jump straight to that bucket — roughly one step. Tree search: to find 7 in a binary search tree, start at the root; if 7 is less than the node go left, if greater go right, repeating until you land on 7 or fall off the tree. On a balanced tree of n values that takes about log2(n) comparisons.

Reading rows:

SELECT * FROM users;
SELECT name FROM users WHERE age >= 18;

The first returns every column of every row; the second returns just the name column for adults. Adding a row:

INSERT INTO users (name, age) VALUES ('Alice', 30);

Changing rows, with a filter so you do not touch everyone:

UPDATE users SET age = 31 WHERE name = 'Alice';

Removing rows:

DELETE FROM users WHERE name = 'Alice';

Dropping the WHERE clause on that last statement would delete every row in the table — a classic and costly slip.
